{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x using Horner's method.\n    Coefficients are in increasing order: xs[0] + xs[1]*x + ... + xs[n]*x^n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not xs:\n        return 0.0\n\n    result = 0.0\n    for i in range(len(xs) - 1, -1, -1):\n        result = result * x + xs[i]\n    return result\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds one real root for a polynomial with guaranteed existence (odd degree, non-zero leading coeff).\n    Uses a geometric bracket search followed by bisection to find x such that poly(x) is near zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Constants for bisection, derived from design specification's NFRs.\n    DEFAULT_TOL_VAL = 1e-10  # Tolerance for absolute value of f(x)\n    DEFAULT_TOL_X = 1e-12    # Tolerance for interval width\n    MAX_BISECTION_ITER = 1000\n    MAX_BRACKET_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE = 1e6 # Max absolute value for bracket search bounds\n\n    # 1. Bracket search: Geometric expansion from origin\n    # The problem statement guarantees a root exists for the given polynomial type.\n    # Therefore, a bracket [a,b] with poly(a)*poly(b) < 0 is expected to be found.\n\n    a_bracket_found = 0.0\n    b_bracket_found = 0.0\n\n    # Test at x = 0.0 first. If it's a root, return immediately.\n    fa_bracket = poly(xs, 0.0)\n    if abs(fa_bracket) < DEFAULT_TOL_VAL:\n        return 0.0\n\n    current_val_magnitude = 1.0\n    while current_val_magnitude <= MAX_BRACKET_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE:\n        # Check positive side relative to 0.0\n        b_pos = current_val_magnitude\n        fb_pos = poly(xs, b_pos)\n        if fa_bracket * fb_pos < 0:\n            a_bracket_found = 0.0 if fa_bracket < 0 else b_pos\n            b_bracket_found = b_pos if fa_bracket < 0 else 0.0\n            break\n        \n        # Check negative side relative to 0.0\n        b_neg = -current_val_magnitude\n        fb_neg = poly(xs, b_neg)\n        if fa_bracket * fb_neg < 0:\n            a_bracket_found = b_neg if fa_bracket < 0 else 0.0\n            b_bracket_found = 0.0 if fa_bracket < 0 else b_neg\n            break\n        \n        current_val_magnitude *= 10.0\n    else: \n        # If no bracket found after exhaustive search (should not happen for guaranteed roots)\n        # Default to a wide range to allow bisection to run, as no exceptions are allowed.\n        a_bracket_found = -MAX_BRACKET_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE\n        b_bracket_found = MAX_BRACKET_SEARCH_MAGNITUDE\n    \n    # 2. Bisection method\n    low_bound = min(a_bracket_found, b_bracket_found)\n    high_bound = max(a_bracket_found, b_bracket_found)\n\n    for _ in range(MAX_BISECTION_ITER):\n        mid = (low_bound + high_bound) / 2.0\n        val_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        # Check for convergence based on function value or interval width.\n        if abs(val_mid) < DEFAULT_TOL_VAL or (high_bound - low_bound) < DEFAULT_TOL_X:\n            return mid\n\n        val_low = poly(xs, low_bound)\n        \n        # Determine the next interval for bisection.\n        if val_low * val_mid < 0:\n            high_bound = mid\n        else:\n            low_bound = mid\n            \n    # If maximum iterations are reached without convergence, return the last midpoint.\n    # (As per critical requirements, no exceptions are allowed for non-convergence).\n    return (low_bound + high_bound) / 2.0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "def sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    # Identify elements at indices i where i % 3 == 0\n    # Create a new list to hold the modified elements, preserving original list type.\n    out = list(l)\n    \n    # Extract values from target indices for sorting\n    values_to_sort = []\n    indices_to_replace = []\n    \n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            indices_to_replace.append(i)\n            values_to_sort.append(l[i])\n            \n    # Sort the extracted values\n    sorted_values = sorted(values_to_sort)\n    \n    # Place the sorted values back into the new list at their original positions\n    for i, original_index in enumerate(indices_to_replace):\n        out[original_index] = sorted_values[i]\n        \n    return out"}
