{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "def strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of integers in a min, max, next-min, next-max pattern.\"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    # Sort the list ascending first.\n    sorted_list = sorted(lst)\n\n    result = []\n    left_ptr = 0\n    right_ptr = len(sorted_list) - 1\n    take_min = True\n\n    # Use two pointers to build the result list by alternating between min and max.\n    while left_ptr <= right_ptr:\n        if take_min:\n            result.append(sorted_list[left_ptr])\n            left_ptr += 1\n        else:\n            result.append(sorted_list[right_ptr])\n            right_ptr -= 1\n        take_min = not take_min\n    \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "def strange_sort_list(lst):\n    '''\n    Given list of integers, return list in strange order.\n    Strange sorting, is when you start with the minimum value,\n    then maximum of the remaining integers, then minimum and so on.\n\n    Examples:\n    strange_sort_list([1, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 4, 2, 3]\n    strange_sort_list([5, 5, 5, 5]) == [5, 5, 5, 5]\n    strange_sort_list([]) == []\n    '''\n    sorted_values = sorted(lst)\n    result = []\n    low, high = 0, len(sorted_values) - 1\n\n    while low <= high:\n        result.append(sorted_values[low])\n        low += 1\n        if low <= high:\n            result.append(sorted_values[high])\n            high -= 1\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "import math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater\n    than the third side.\n    Example:\n    triangle_area(3, 4, 5) == 6.00\n    triangle_area(1, 2, 10) == -1\n    '''\n    # Check if the three sides form a valid triangle using the triangle inequality.\n    # This also implicitly handles non-positive side lengths as they would violate the inequality.\n    if not ((a + b > c) and (a + c > b) and (b + c > a)):\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate the semi-perimeter (half the perimeter).\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n\n    # Calculate the area using Heron's formula.\n    # The term under the square root should be non-negative; max(0.0, ...) handles tiny negative floats due to precision errors.\n    area_squared_term = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n    area = math.sqrt(max(0.0, area_squared_term))\n\n    # Round the calculated area to two decimal places. A small epsilon helps ensure correct rounding for boundary values.\n    return round(area + 1e-12, 2)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "import math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater\n    than the third side.\n    Example:\n    triangle_area(3, 4, 5) == 6.00\n    triangle_area(1, 2, 10) == -1\n    '''\n    side_a = float(a)\n    side_b = float(b)\n    side_c = float(c)\n\n    # Sides must be positive.\n    if not (side_a > 0 and side_b > 0 and side_c > 0):\n        return -1.0\n    \n    # Check triangle inequality.\n    if not (side_a + side_b > side_c and\n            side_a + side_c > side_b and\n            side_b + side_c > side_a):\n        return -1.0\n\n    s = (side_a + side_b + side_c) / 2.0\n    \n    area_squared = s * (s - side_a) * (s - side_b) * (s - side_c)\n    \n    # Handle floating point inaccuracies for near-degenerate triangles.\n    if area_squared < 0 and area_squared > -1e-12:\n        area_squared = 0.0\n    \n    # If still negative, it's an invalid case.\n    if area_squared < 0:\n        return -1.0\n        \n    area = math.sqrt(area_squared)\n\n    return round(area, 2)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/72", "solution": "def will_it_fly(q,w):\n    '''\n    Write a function that returns True if the object q will fly, and False otherwise.\n    The object q will fly if it's balanced (it is a palindromic list) and the sum of its elements is less than or equal the maximum possible weight w.\n    '''\n    is_palindromic = (q == q[::-1])\n    total_weight = sum(q)\n    is_within_limit = (total_weight <= w)\n    return is_palindromic and is_within_limit"}
