{"task_id": "HumanEval/96", "solution": "def count_up_to(n):\n    \"\"\"Computes all prime numbers strictly less than n using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\n    Returns an empty list for n <= 2.\"\"\"\n    import math\n\n    if n <= 2:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize sieve: True for potentially prime (represented by 1), False for not prime (0).\n    sieve = bytearray(b\"\\x01\") * n\n    sieve[0:2] = b\"\\x00\\x00\"  # 0 and 1 are not prime\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of n.\n    for p in range(2, int(math.isqrt(n)) + 1):\n        # If p is still marked as prime\n        if sieve[p]:\n            # Mark all multiples of p as not prime, starting from p*p.\n            start = p * p\n            if start < n:\n                # Efficiently set a slice of the bytearray to zeros.\n                count = (n - 1 - start) // p + 1\n                sieve[start:n:p] = b\"\\x00\" * count\n\n    # Collect all numbers whose index is still marked as prime.\n    return [i for i, is_prime in enumerate(sieve) if is_prime]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/98", "solution": "def count_upper(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string s, count the number of uppercase vowels in even indices.\n    \n    For example:\n    count_upper('aBCdEf') returns 1\n    count_upper('abcdefg') returns 0\n    count_upper('dBBE') returns 0\n    \"\"\"\n    vowels = {'A', 'E', 'I', 'O', 'U'}\n    count = 0\n    for i, char in enumerate(s):\n        if i % 2 == 0 and char in vowels:\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "from decimal import Decimal, localcontext, ROUND_FLOOR, ROUND_CEILING\n\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Create a function that takes a value (string) representing a number\n    and returns the closest integer to it. If the number is equidistant\n    from two integers, round it away from zero.\n\n    Examples\n    >>> closest_integer(\"10\")\n    10\n    >>> closest_integer(\"15.3\")\n    15\n\n    Note:\n    Rounding away from zero means that if the given number is equidistant\n    from two integers, the one you should return is the one that is the\n    farthest from zero. For example closest_integer(\"14.5\") should\n    return 15 and closest_integer(\"-14.5\") should return -15.\n    '''\n    # Normalize input and parse the string into a Decimal number using a high precision context.\n    text = value.strip()\n    with localcontext() as ctx:\n        ctx.prec = 28\n        number = Decimal(text)\n\n    # Compute the floor and ceiling integers.\n    floor_i = int(number.to_integral_value(rounding=ROUND_FLOOR))\n    ceil_i = int(number.to_integral_value(rounding=ROUND_CEILING))\n\n    # Calculate distances to both integers.\n    dist_floor = abs(number - floor_i)\n    dist_ceil = abs(ceil_i - number)\n\n    # Apply away-from-zero tie-breaking or choose the nearest integer.\n    if dist_floor == dist_ceil:\n        return ceil_i if number >= 0 else floor_i\n    else:\n        return floor_i if dist_floor < dist_ceil else ceil_i"}
