{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import math\n\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Returns the closest integer to a numeric string value, rounding ties away from zero.\n    This means if equidistant, the integer farther from zero is chosen.\n    '''\n    f = float(value)\n\n    floor_v = math.floor(f)\n    ceil_v = math.ceil(f)\n\n    diff_floor = abs(f - floor_v)\n    diff_ceil = abs(ceil_v - f)\n\n    if diff_floor < diff_ceil:\n        return int(floor_v)\n    elif diff_ceil < diff_floor:\n        return int(ceil_v)\n    else: # It's a tie, f is equidistant from floor_v and ceil_v\n        # Round away from zero: for positive f, pick ceil_v; for negative f, pick floor_v.\n        return int(ceil_v if f >= 0 else floor_v)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/98", "solution": "```\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"count-upper-app\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"A Python library and CLI for counting uppercase vowels at even indices.\"\nauthors = [{ name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" }]\ndependencies = [] # No runtime dependencies beyond standard library\n\n[project.optional-dependencies]\ndev = [\"pytest~=8.0\"]\n\n[project.scripts]\ncount-upper = \"count_upper_app.main:main\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\npythonpath = \"src\" # Allows importing count_upper_app from tests without explicit path modifications\naddopts = \"--cov=count_upper_app --cov-report=term-missing\" # Enables coverage reports\n\n```\n\n```markdown\n# README.md\n\n## Count Upper App\n\nA Python library and command-line interface (CLI) for counting uppercase vowels at even indices in a given string.\n\n### Features\n\n-   **Library API**: Easily integrate the counting logic into your Python applications.\n-   **CLI Tool**: A simple command-line utility for quick checks.\n-   **Persistence**: Optionally store query results (input string, result, timestamp) to a local JSON Lines file for history or debugging.\n-   **Automated Tests**: Comprehensive unit and integration tests to ensure correctness and reliability.\n\n### Installation\n\n1.  **Clone the repository:**\n    ```bash\n    git clone https://github.com/your-username/count-upper-app.git\n    cd count-upper-app\n    ```\n2.  **Install in editable mode (for development):**\n    ```bash\n    pip install -e .\n    ```\n    This will install the package and make the `count-upper` command available in your terminal.\n\n### Usage\n\n#### Library API\n\nYou can import and use the `count_upper` function directly in your Python code:\n\n```python\nfrom count_upper_app import count_upper\nfrom count_upper_app.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\ntry:\n    result = count_upper(\"aBCdEf\")\n    print(f\"Result: {result}\")  # Output: Result: 1\n\n    result = count_upper(\"abcdefg\")\n    print(f\"Result: {result}\")  # Output: Result: 0\n\n    result = count_upper(\"APPLE\") # A at 0, P at 1, P at 2, L at 3, E at 4\n    print(f\"Result: {result}\")  # Output: Result: 2 (A and E)\n\n    result = count_upper(\"dBBE\") # d at 0, B at 1, B at 2, E at 3\n    print(f\"Result: {result}\")  # Output: Result: 0\n\n    count_upper(123) # This will raise InvalidInputError\nexcept InvalidInputError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n#### CLI Tool\n\nRun the `count-upper` command from your terminal:\n\n```bash\n# Basic usage\ncount-upper \"aBCdEf\"\n# Output: 1\n\ncount-upper \"Hello World\"\n# Output: 1 (E at index 1 is not even. O at index 4 is even.)\n\n# With persistence enabled\ncount-upper \"AnotherExample\" --persist\n# Output: 3 (A at 0, O at 6, E at 8)\n# This will save the input and result to 'data/results.jsonl'\n\n# Specify a custom persistence path\ncount-upper \"CustomPathTest\" --persist --persist-path my_results.jsonl\n# Output: 3 (u at 1 is not even. o at 4 is even. e at 7 is not even.)\n```\n\n### Development\n\n#### Running Tests\n\nTo run the automated tests and check code coverage:\n\n```bash\npytest\n```\n\n#### Project Structure\n\nThe project follows a modular design as specified:\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 count_upper_app/\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 main.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 controllers.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 services.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 models.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 repository.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 utils.py\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 exceptions.py\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 config.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 data/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 (optional) results.jsonl\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 tests/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 test_services.py\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 test_controllers.py\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 test_repository.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md\n```\n\n### Persistence Details\n\nResults are persisted to a JSON Lines file (default: `data/results.jsonl`). Each line in the file is a JSON object representing a `ResultRecord`.\n\nExample record:\n```json\n{\"id\": \"e7b8a1f4-3d3b-4c9d-95a6-1a2b3c4d5e6f\", \"input\": \"aBCdEf\", \"result\": 1, \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-12T12:34:56Z\"}\n```\n\nThe `data/` directory and `results.jsonl` file will be created automatically if they don't exist when persistence is enabled.\n\n```\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/exceptions.py\n\"\"\"\nCustom exception definitions for the count_upper_app.\n\"\"\"\n\nclass InvalidInputError(TypeError):\n    \"\"\"\n    Exception raised for invalid input types or values.\n    It's a subclass of TypeError for semantic clarity regarding input type issues.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/config.py\n\"\"\"\nConfiguration constants for the count_upper_app.\n\"\"\"\nfrom typing import Set\n\nDEFAULT_PERSIST_PATH: str = \"data/results.jsonl\"\nDEFAULT_VOWELS: Set[str] = {\"A\", \"E\", \"I\", \"O\", \"U\"}\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/utils.py\n\"\"\"\nUtility functions and helpers for the count_upper_app.\n\"\"\"\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom .config import DEFAULT_VOWELS # Use config for consistency, as per design intent.\n\ndef is_uppercase_vowel(ch: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a character is a single-character uppercase vowel (A, E, I, O, U).\n    \"\"\"\n    return isinstance(ch, str) and len(ch) == 1 and ch in DEFAULT_VOWELS\n\ndef now_iso8601() -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the current UTC time in ISO8601 string format.\n    \"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec='seconds') + 'Z'\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/models.py\n\"\"\"\nData Transfer Objects (DTOs) and data models for the count_upper_app.\n\"\"\"\nfrom typing import Dict, Any\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\nclass InputString:\n    \"\"\"\n    Represents an input string, providing validation.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def validate(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Ensures the stored value is a non-null string.\n        Raises InvalidInputError if validation fails.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.value, str):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n        # Additional checks (e.g., length limits) could be added here if required.\n\nclass ResultRecord:\n    \"\"\"\n    Represents a single computation result, suitable for persistence.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, id: str, input: str, result: int, timestamp: str):\n        self.id = id\n        self.input = input\n        self.result = result\n        self.timestamp = timestamp\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Converts the ResultRecord object to a dictionary.\n        \"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"id\": self.id,\n            \"input\": self.input,\n            \"result\": self.result,\n            \"timestamp\": self.timestamp,\n        }\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/repository.py\n\"\"\"\nRepository interfaces and implementations for persisting results.\n\"\"\"\nimport abc\nimport json\nimport os\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\nfrom .models import ResultRecord\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass RepositoryInterface(abc.ABC):\n    \"\"\"\n    Abstract Base Class for repositories handling ResultRecord objects.\n    \"\"\"\n    @abc.abstractmethod\n    def save(self, record: ResultRecord) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Saves a ResultRecord and returns its ID.\n        \"\"\"\n        pass\n\n    @abc.abstractmethod\n    def list(self, limit: int = 100) -> List[ResultRecord]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieves a list of ResultRecord objects, up to a specified limit.\n        \"\"\"\n        pass\n\nclass InMemoryRepository(RepositoryInterface):\n    \"\"\"\n    An in-memory implementation of the RepositoryInterface for testing or non-persistent storage.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._storage: List[ResultRecord] = []\n\n    def save(self, record: ResultRecord) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Appends a ResultRecord to the in-memory list.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._storage.append(record)\n        return record.id\n\n    def list(self, limit: int = 100) -> List[ResultRecord]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the last `limit` records from the in-memory storage.\n        \"\"\"\n        return self._storage[-limit:]\n\nclass FileBackedRepository(RepositoryInterface):\n    \"\"\"\n    A file-backed implementation of the RepositoryInterface using JSON Lines format.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, file_path: str):\n        self.file_path = file_path\n        # Ensure the directory for the file_path exists\n        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(file_path) or '.', exist_ok=True)\n\n    def save(self, record: ResultRecord) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Serializes a ResultRecord to a JSON line and appends it to the file.\n        Raises IOError on file write errors.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            line = json.dumps(record.to_dict())\n            with open(self.file_path, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                f.write(line + \"\\n\")\n            return record.id\n        except IOError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Failed to save record to {self.file_path}: {e}\")\n            raise # Propagate the error for the caller to handle (log and continue as per spec)\n        except TypeError as e: # Catch json.dumps serialization errors\n            logger.error(f\"Failed to serialize record {record.id}: {e}\")\n            raise # Propagate\n\n    def list(self, limit: int = 100) -> List[ResultRecord]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Reads the last `limit` JSON lines from the file, parses them, and returns ResultRecord objects.\n        \"\"\"\n        records: List[ResultRecord] = []\n        try:\n            with open(self.file_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n                # Read all lines and then take the last 'limit' for efficiency on small files.\n                # For very large files, a more efficient tail-reading approach would be needed.\n                lines = f.readlines()\n                for line in lines[-limit:]:\n                    try:\n                        data: Dict[str, Any] = json.loads(line.strip())\n                        records.append(\n                            ResultRecord(\n                                id=data[\"id\"],\n                                input=data[\"input\"],\n                                result=data[\"result\"],\n                                timestamp=data[\"timestamp\"],\n                            )\n                        )\n                    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n                        logger.warning(f\"Skipping malformed JSON line in {self.file_path}: {e} - Line: {line.strip()}\")\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            logger.info(f\"Persistence file not found: {self.file_path}. Returning empty list.\")\n        except IOError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Error reading persistence file {self.file_path}: {e}\")\n        return records\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/services.py\n\"\"\"\nService layer containing the core business logic for string analysis.\n\"\"\"\nfrom typing import Set\nfrom .utils import is_uppercase_vowel\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\nfrom .config import DEFAULT_VOWELS # Use config for consistency\n\nclass StringAnalyzer:\n    \"\"\"\n    Implements the core algorithm to count uppercase vowels at even indices.\n    \"\"\"\n    # DEFAULT_VOWELS is now defined in config.py and used by is_uppercase_vowel\n    # This class directly depends on the helper, not directly on DEFAULT_VOWELS\n    # The snippet had DEFAULT_VOWELS here, but config.py is preferred as per design.\n\n    def count_upper_vowels_even_indices(self, s: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the number of uppercase vowels (A, E, I, O, U)\n        occurring at even indices (0-based) in a given string.\n\n        Args:\n            s: The input string to analyze.\n\n        Returns:\n            The count of matching characters.\n\n        Raises:\n            InvalidInputError: If the input `s` is not a string.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(s, str):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n        count = 0\n        for idx, ch in enumerate(s):\n            if (idx % 2) == 0 and is_uppercase_vowel(ch):\n                count += 1\n        return count\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/controllers.py\n\"\"\"\nController layer, orchestrating interaction between CLI, services, and repositories.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nimport uuid\nimport logging\nfrom typing import Optional\n\nfrom .models import InputString, ResultRecord\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\nfrom .services import StringAnalyzer\nfrom .repository import RepositoryInterface, InMemoryRepository\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"\n    Orchestrates validation, computation, and optional persistence for CLI requests.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, repository: Optional[RepositoryInterface] = None, analyzer: Optional[StringAnalyzer] = None):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the CLIController with optional injected dependencies.\n        Defaults to InMemoryRepository if no repository is provided.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.repository = repository # Can be None if persistence is not enabled in main\n        self.analyzer = analyzer or StringAnalyzer()\n\n    def run(self, input_str: str, persist: bool = False) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Executes the main logic for a CLI command: validates, computes,\n        optionally persists, prints result, and handles errors.\n\n        Args:\n            input_str: The string provided by the user.\n            persist: A boolean indicating whether to persist the result.\n\n        Returns:\n            An integer exit code (0 for success, 2 for invalid input).\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            InputString(input_str).validate()\n            result = self.analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(input_str)\n\n            # Import here to avoid circular dependency if utils imports models\n            from .utils import now_iso8601 \n            record = ResultRecord(str(uuid.uuid4()), input_str, result, now_iso8601())\n\n            if persist and self.repository:\n                try:\n                    self.repository.save(record)\n                    logger.info(f\"Result persisted: {record.id}\")\n                except Exception as e:\n                    logger.error(f\"Failed to persist result record {record.id}: {e}\")\n                    # As per spec: log and continue, do not fail the count operation.\n\n            print(result) # Print the numeric result to stdout\n            return 0 # Success exit code\n        except InvalidInputError as e:\n            print(f\"Error: Invalid input: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2 # Invalid input exit code\n        except Exception as e:\n            # Catch any other unexpected errors during analysis or record creation\n            print(f\"Error: An unexpected error occurred: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            logger.exception(\"An unexpected error occurred in CLIController.run\")\n            return 1 # General error exit code\n\n    # The _handle_success and _handle_error methods were described in design,\n    # but the example snippet for `run` implements the printing and exit code\n    # directly within `run`. Following the snippet for implementation simplicity.\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/__init__.py\n\"\"\"\nPublic API for the count_upper_app library.\n\"\"\"\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .services import StringAnalyzer\nfrom .models import InputString, ResultRecord\nfrom .exceptions import InvalidInputError\nfrom .repository import RepositoryInterface, InMemoryRepository, FileBackedRepository # Export for advanced programmatic use\n\n# Initialize a default analyzer for the public API function\n_analyzer_api_instance = StringAnalyzer()\n\ndef count_upper(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of uppercase vowels (A, E, I, O, U)\n    at even indices (0-based) in the given string.\n\n    This function serves as the primary programmatic entry point for the library.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The count of matching characters.\n\n    Raises:\n        InvalidInputError: If `s` is not a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    # InputString handles validation and raises InvalidInputError if needed.\n    InputString(s).validate()\n    return _analyzer_api_instance.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(s)\n\n# Optionally, if more advanced API access to repositories is desired,\n# you could expose a function that accepts an optional repository\n# or a full Application class. For now, sticking to the simple `count_upper`.\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"count_upper\",\n    \"InvalidInputError\",\n    \"StringAnalyzer\",\n    \"InputString\",\n    \"ResultRecord\",\n    \"RepositoryInterface\",\n    \"InMemoryRepository\",\n    \"FileBackedRepository\",\n]\n\n```\n\n```python\n# src/count_upper_app/main.py\n\"\"\"\nCLI entrypoint for the count_upper_app.\nHandles argument parsing, controller instantiation, and execution.\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .controllers import CLIController\nfrom .repository import FileBackedRepository\nfrom .config import DEFAULT_PERSIST_PATH\nfrom .services import StringAnalyzer # Explicitly import for potential Analyzer injection\n\ndef _parse_args(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> argparse.Namespace:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses command-line arguments using argparse.\n    \"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        prog=\"count-upper\",\n        description=\"Count uppercase vowels at even indices in a string.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"input_str\",\n        type=str,\n        help=\"The string to analyze.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--persist\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Enable persistence of query results to a file.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--persist-path\",\n        type=str,\n        default=DEFAULT_PERSIST_PATH,\n        help=f\"Path to the JSON Lines file for persistence (default: {DEFAULT_PERSIST_PATH}).\"\n    )\n    return parser.parse_args(argv)\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Main entrypoint for the command-line interface.\n\n    Parses arguments, sets up the repository and controller,\n    and runs the counting operation. Prints output to stdout/stderr.\n\n    Args:\n        argv: Optional list of arguments (defaults to sys.argv[1:]).\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer exit code (0 for success, non-zero for errors).\n    \"\"\"\n    args = _parse_args(argv)\n\n    repository = None\n    if args.persist:\n        repository = FileBackedRepository(args.persist_path)\n    \n    # StringAnalyzer is stateless, so we can create one directly.\n    # In more complex apps, this might come from a DI container.\n    analyzer = StringAnalyzer()\n\n    controller = CLIController(repository=repository, analyzer=analyzer)\n    return controller.run(args.input_str, persist=args.persist)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Standard entry point for a console script\n    sys.exit(main())\n\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_services.py\n\"\"\"\nUnit tests for the StringAnalyzer service.\n\"\"\"\nimport pytest\nfrom count_upper_app.services import StringAnalyzer\nfrom count_upper_app.exceptions import InvalidInputError\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef analyzer():\n    \"\"\"Provides a StringAnalyzer instance for tests.\"\"\"\n    return StringAnalyzer()\n\ndef test_examples(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests specified examples from the requirements.\"\"\"\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"aBCdEf\") == 1 # E at index 4\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"abcdefg\") == 0\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"dBBE\") == 0 # No uppercase vowel at even index\n\ndef test_empty_string(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests an empty input string.\"\"\"\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"\") == 0\n\ndef test_all_uppercase_vowels_even_indices(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests strings with uppercase vowels exclusively at even indices.\"\"\"\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"A\") == 1\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"AEIOU\") == 3 # A(0), I(2), U(4)\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"AaEeIiOoUu\") == 5 # A(0), E(2), I(4), O(6), U(8)\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"A_E_I_O_U\") == 5 # A(0), E(2), I(4), O(6), U(8)\n\ndef test_all_uppercase_vowels_odd_indices(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests strings with uppercase vowels exclusively at odd indices.\"\"\"\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"_A_E_I_O_U\") == 0\n\ndef test_mixed_case_and_non_vowels(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests strings with a mix of characters and cases.\"\"\"\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"PyThOn PrOgRaMmInG\") == 1 # O at index 6\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"TESTING\") == 1 # E at index 2\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"AEIOUaeiouAEIOU\") == 6 # A(0), I(2), U(4), A(10), I(12), U(14)\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"CRyPtO\") == 1 # O at index 5 is not even. Y at 2 is not vowel.\n\ndef test_no_vowels(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests strings containing no vowels.\"\"\"\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"BCDFG\") == 0\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"bcdfgh\") == 0\n    assert analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(\"12345!@#$\") == 0\n\ndef test_invalid_input_type(analyzer):\n    \"\"\"Tests that non-string inputs raise InvalidInputError.\"\"\"\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Input must be a string.\"):\n        analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(None)\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Input must be a string.\"):\n        analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(123)\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidInputError, match=\"Input must be a string.\"):\n        analyzer.count_upper_vowels_even_indices(['a', 'B'])\n\n```\n\n```python\n# tests/test_repository.py\n\"\"\"\nTests for repository implementations (InMemoryRepository, FileBackedRepository).\n\"\"\"\nimport pytest\nimport os\nimport json\nimport uuid\nimport tempfile\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\nfrom count_upper_app.models import ResultRecord\nfrom count_upper_app.repository import RepositoryInterface, InMemoryRepository, FileBackedRepository\nfrom count_upper_app.utils import now_iso8601 # For predictable timestamps\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef sample_records() -> List[ResultRecord]:\n    \"\"\"Provides a list of sample ResultRecord objects.\"\"\"\n    return [\n        ResultRecord(str(uuid.uuid4()), \"Input1\", 1, now_iso8601()),\n        ResultRecord(str(uuid.uuid4()), \"Input2\", 2, now_iso8601()),\n        ResultRecord(str(uuid.uuid4()), \"Input3\", 3, now_iso8601()),\n    ]\n\nclass TestInMemoryRepository:\n    \"\"\"Tests for InMemoryRepository.\"\"\"\n    @pytest.fixture\n    def repo(self):\n        \"\"\"Provides a fresh InMemoryRepository.\"\"\"\n        return InMemoryRepository()\n\n    def test_save_and_list(self, repo, sample_records):\n        \"\"\"Tests saving and listing multiple records.\"\"\"\n        ids = [repo.save(r) for r in sample_records]\n        assert len(ids) == len(sample_records)\n        assert all(isinstance(id_, str) for id_ in ids)\n\n        listed_records = repo.list()\n        assert len(listed_records) == len(sample_records)\n        assert listed_records == sample_records # In-memory comparison\n\n    def test_list_limit(self, repo, sample_records):\n        \"\"\"Tests the limit parameter for listing records.\"\"\"\n        for r in sample_records:\n            repo.save(r)\n\n        listed_records = repo.list(limit=2)\n        assert len(listed_records) == 2\n        assert listed_records == sample_records[1:] # Last two records\n\n        listed_records_all = repo.list(limit=5) # Limit greater than available\n        assert len(listed_records_all) == len(sample_records)\n        assert listed_records_all == sample_records\n\n    def test_empty_repo_list(self, repo):\n        \"\"\"Tests listing from an empty repository.\"\"\"\n        assert repo.list() == []\n\nclass TestFileBackedRepository:\n    \"\"\"Tests for FileBackedRepository.\"\"\"\n    @pytest.fixture\n    def temp_jsonl_file(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Creates a temporary JSON Lines file and ensures its cleanup.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Create a temporary directory instead of just a file to simulate data/ structure\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmpdir:\n            file_path = os.path.join(tmpdir, \"test_results.jsonl\")\n            yield file_path\n        # TemporaryDirectory handles cleanup automatically\n\n    @pytest.fixture\n    def repo(self, temp_jsonl_file):\n        \"\"\"Provides a FileBackedRepository instance pointing to a temporary file.\"\"\"\n        return FileBackedRepository(temp_jsonl_file)\n\n    def test_save_creates_file_and_content(self, repo, sample_records):\n        \"\"\"Tests that save creates the file and writes correct JSON content.\"\"\"\n        repo.save(sample_records[0])\n        assert os.path.exists(repo.file_path)\n\n        with open(repo.file_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            content = f.read().strip()\n            expected_json = json.dumps(sample_records[0].to_dict())\n            assert content == expected_json\n\n    def test_save_appends_to_file(self, repo, sample_records):\n        \"\"\"Tests that subsequent saves append new lines to the file.\"\"\"\n        for r in sample_records:\n            repo.save(r)\n\n        with open(repo.file_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            lines = f.readlines()\n            assert len(lines) == len(sample_records)\n            for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n                assert json.loads(line.strip()) == sample_records[i].to_dict()\n\n    def test_list_reads_correctly(self, repo, sample_records):\n        \"\"\"Tests that list reads records correctly from the file.\"\"\"\n        for r in sample_records:\n            repo.save(r)\n\n        listed_records = repo.list()\n        assert len(listed_records) == len(sample_records)\n        for i, record in enumerate(listed_records):\n            assert record.id == sample_records[i].id\n            assert record.input == sample_records[i].input\n            assert record.result == sample_records[i].result\n            assert record.timestamp == sample_records[i].timestamp\n\n    def test_list_from_nonexistent_file(self, repo):\n        \"\"\"Tests listing from a file that does not exist.\"\"\"\n        # Ensure the file doesn't exist before testing\n        if os.path.exists(repo.file_path):\n            os.remove(repo.file_path)\n        assert repo.list() == []\n\n    def test_list_limit_from_file(self, repo, sample_records):\n        \"\"\"Tests the list limit parameter for file-backed repository.\"\"\"\n        for r in sample_records:\n            repo.save(r)\n\n        listed_records = repo.list(limit=2)\n        assert len(listed_records) == 2\n        # File-backed list returns the last 'limit' records\n        assert listed_records[0].id == sample_records[1].id\n     