{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "```python\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a positive integer to its lowercase Roman numeral equivalent.\n    Assumes input `number` is an integer between 1 and 1000 inclusive.\n    \"\"\"\n    ROMAN_NUMERAL_MAP = [\n        (1000, \"M\"), (900, \"CM\"), (500, \"D\"), (400, \"CD\"),\n        (100, \"C\"), (90, \"XC\"), (50, \"L\"), (40, \"XL\"),\n        (10, \"X\"), (9, \"IX\"), (5, \"V\"), (4, \"IV\"), (1, \"I\")\n    ]\n\n    result = []\n    n = number\n    for value, token in ROMAN_NUMERAL_MAP:\n        while n >= value:\n            result.append(token)\n            n -= value\n    return \"\".join(result).lower()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "def right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which one angle is right angle or \n    90 degree.\n    Example:\n    right_angle_triangle(3, 4, 5) == True\n    right_angle_triangle(1, 2, 3) == False\n    '''\n    # Sort the sides to identify the two shorter sides and the longest side (hypotenuse).\n    sides = sorted([a, b, c])\n    side1, side2, hypotenuse = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n\n    # Calculate squares of the sides.\n    side1_sq = side1 * side1\n    side2_sq = side2 * side2\n    hypotenuse_sq = hypotenuse * hypotenuse\n\n    # Define a small epsilon for floating-point comparison, as specified in the design.\n    epsilon = 1e-9\n\n    # Check if the sum of squares of the two shorter sides is approximately equal to the hypotenuse squared.\n    return abs((side1_sq + side2_sq) - hypotenuse_sq) < epsilon"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "```python\ndef right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if three side lengths form a right-angled triangle.\n    Uses a small fixed tolerance for floating-point comparisons.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_close_internal(val1, val2, tolerance):\n        return abs(val1 - val2) <= tolerance\n\n    sides = sorted([a, b, c])\n    x, y, z = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n\n    DEFAULT_TOLERANCE = 1e-9\n\n    return _is_close_internal(x*x + y*y, z*z, DEFAULT_TOLERANCE)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Returns the word with the maximum number of unique characters.\n    In case of a tie, the lexicographically smallest word is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    max_unique_chars = -1\n    result_word = \"\"\n\n    for word in words:\n        current_unique_chars = len(set(word))\n\n        if current_unique_chars > max_unique_chars:\n            max_unique_chars = current_unique_chars\n            result_word = word\n        elif current_unique_chars == max_unique_chars:\n            result_word = min(result_word, word)\n\n    return result_word"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "```python\ndef find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Return the word with the maximum number of unique characters from a list.\n    If multiple words tie, return the one that comes first lexicographically.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    max_unique_count = -1\n    result_word = \"\"\n\n    for word in words:\n        current_unique_count = len(set(word))\n\n        if current_unique_count > max_unique_count:\n            max_unique_count = current_unique_count\n            result_word = word\n        elif current_unique_count == max_unique_count:\n            # Tie-breaking: choose the lexicographically smallest\n            if word < result_word:\n                result_word = word\n\n    return result_word\n```"}
